// "Why it works": the written explanation behind each key-fact block, shown
// first when somebody presses Explain further.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// WHY THIS EXISTS
//
// Explain further (lib/explainFurther.ts) opens what the site had already
// written — the definitions, the common mistake, the worked example — and the
// one thing it could not do was answer *why* a fact is true: 92% of the 6,291
// key-fact bullets state a fact and never say why. This is that missing piece,
// one paragraph per block, written for the student who missed the lesson.
//
// Biology first (2026-11-11), as Matthew chose, so he can judge the voice
// before the other fourteen subjects are written.
//
// ⚠️ KEYED BY THE BLOCK'S EXACT HEADING. Rename a heading and its paragraph
// stops showing, so check-content fails if any heading here matches no block,
// and if any biology block has no paragraph.
//
// Data only: no imports, read by scripts/check-content.mjs with plain Node.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/** "subject/topic" → block heading → the explanation (one paragraph, or two split by a blank line). */
export const WHY: Record<string, Record<string, string>> = {
  "biology/cell-structure": {
    "Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells":
      "The whole split comes down to whether the DNA is wrapped in its own membrane. A eukaryotic cell keeps its DNA inside a nucleus and also has parts such as mitochondria, which prokaryotes lack. A bacterium has none of that internal structure, so its single loop of DNA simply sits in the cytoplasm, and the whole cell can be a tenth of the size of a eukaryotic cell or less. Plasmids are extras, which means a bacterium can carry a few useful genes separately from its main loop. The cell wall shows why one feature is not enough to classify a cell: bacteria and plants both need a wall for support, but they build it from different materials.",
    "The parts of an animal cell":
      "Each part exists because the cell has to run itself, and together they form a chain. The nucleus holds the DNA, and DNA carries the instructions for making proteins. That is why the nucleus controls the cell, because proteins such as enzymes decide which reactions happen. Those proteins are actually assembled on the ribosomes, out in the cytoplasm. Most reactions take place in the cytoplasm because it is a watery jelly in which dissolved substances can move around and meet. Many of those reactions need energy, so mitochondria release it from glucose by aerobic respiration. None of this would work if the cell's contents leaked away or harmful substances flooded in, so the membrane acts as a controlled border, letting oxygen and glucose in and waste such as carbon dioxide out.",
    "What plant cells have as well":
      "Each extra part solves a problem an animal cell does not have. Much of a plant is held up by its cells pressing firmly against each other. Water enters the vacuole by osmosis and pushes the cell contents outwards against the cellulose wall, which pushes back, so the cell becomes firm instead of bursting. Firm cells together keep stems and leaves upright, which is why a plant wilts when it is short of water. Chloroplasts are there because plants make their own glucose by photosynthesis, and the chlorophyll inside them absorbs the light that powers it. That is also why chloroplasts appear only where light reaches, since a root cell would gain nothing from them. Fungi and bacteria have walls too, though not made of cellulose, so a wall on its own never proves a cell is a plant.",
    "Specialised cells":
      "A specialised cell is built around one job, so its features follow from what that job demands. Swimming and contracting both need a lot of energy, which is why sperm and muscle cells are packed with mitochondria to release it by respiration. A nerve cell has to pass a message over long distances and on to many other cells, so it is long and branched. A root hair absorbs water by osmosis and mineral ions by active transport, and a bigger surface means more can be absorbed at once, so the hair sticks out into the soil. Xylem cells are dead and empty because water flows best through an open tube, while phloem must stay alive because moving sugars needs energy. Plant cells keep the ability to differentiate, which is why a cutting can grow into a whole new plant.",
  },
  "biology/cell-transport": {
    "Diffusion":
      "Particles in a liquid or gas are always moving about at random. More particles leave a crowded region than enter it, simply because there are more of them there to move, so the overall effect is a spread from high concentration to low. No energy from the cell is needed because the particles' own movement does the work. A steeper gradient means a bigger imbalance between the two sides, so more particles cross one way than the other each second. A higher temperature gives particles more energy, so they move faster. A larger surface area gives more places to cross at once, and a shorter distance means each particle arrives sooner. The overall spreading stops once the concentrations are equal, which is why the blood keeps carrying oxygen away from the lungs to keep the gradient steep.",
    "Osmosis":
      "Osmosis is really the diffusion of water through a membrane that lets small water molecules pass but holds back larger dissolved particles such as sugar. In a dilute solution more of the molecules are water, so more water molecules hit the membrane and cross from that side than from the concentrated side. The result is an overall flow of water into the more concentrated solution, and the cell spends no energy on it. A plant cell in dilute solution fills with water until its contents press hard against the wall, which pushes back, so the cell becomes firm but cannot burst. In a concentrated solution water leaves, the vacuole shrinks and the membrane can peel away from the wall. An animal cell has nothing to push back, so it can keep taking in water until it bursts.",
    "Active transport":
      "Moving something against its gradient is like pushing it uphill. Left alone it would spread the other way, so the cell has to spend energy to carry it across the membrane. That energy comes from respiration, which is why cells that do a lot of active transport, such as root hair cells and the cells lining the small intestine, are full of mitochondria. Root hairs need it because soil water usually holds fewer mineral ions than the root cells already contain, so diffusion would carry ions out of the root, not in. The gut meets the same problem near the end of digestion. Once the glucose concentration in the gut has fallen below that in the blood, diffusion would start moving glucose back into the gut, so active transport is needed to take up the last of it.",
    "Exchange surfaces":
      "Every substance an organism uses has to come in across its surface, but the demand comes from its volume, because every cell inside needs supplying. As an organism gets bigger its volume grows faster than its surface area, so the ratio falls. Double the side of a cube and its surface area goes up four times, but its volume goes up eight times. A single cell is so small that its surface can supply all of it. In a large animal the surface is far too small for its needs, and inner cells are too far away for diffusion to reach in time. That is why large animals need specialised surfaces such as lungs, gills and villi. A large area gives more room to cross, thinness shortens the distance, and blood flow or ventilation keeps the concentration gradient steep.",
  },
  "biology/cell-division": {
    "Chromosomes and the cell cycle":
      "Mitosis has one aim: each new cell must end up with a complete, exact copy of the genetic instructions. That is why the cell copies its DNA before it divides. After copying, each chromosome exists as two identical copies, so when the copies are pulled to opposite ends of the cell, each end receives a full copy of all 46 chromosomes. The first stage is the longest because the cell has a lot to build. It has to grow and make enough mitochondria, ribosomes and other parts for two cells, or each daughter cell would start out short. Cytokinesis then splits the cytoplasm and membrane so that each new nucleus ends up in its own cell. Because no chromosomes are lost or mixed with anyone else's, the daughter cells are genetically identical to each other and to the parent.",
    "Why cells divide":
      "A single cell cannot keep getting bigger, because it would soon have too little surface for its volume, so an organism grows by making more cells rather than larger ones. Every new cell has to work like the cells around it, and mitosis makes sure of that, because it produces identical copies with the full set of genes. The same exact copying is what makes repair possible: a cut in your skin is closed by new cells carrying the same genes as the ones they replace. Skin, the gut lining and blood cells wear out, so they need replacing for as long as you live. Asexual reproduction uses the same process, which is why the offspring are clones of the parent. Normally division is tightly controlled, and when that control fails, cells keep dividing and pile up into a tumour.",
    "Stem cells":
      "Every cell in your body carries the same genes, but a specialised cell only uses the ones its job needs. A stem cell has not narrowed down yet, which is why it can still become something else. Embryonic stem cells are the most flexible because the embryo still has to make every tissue in the body. Adult stem cells only top up particular tissues, so the ones in bone marrow can make several types of cell, including blood cells, but not every type. Plant meristems stay active for life, which is why a plant keeps growing new shoots and roots. Therapeutic cloning solves a different problem. The immune system attacks cells carrying surface proteins it does not recognise, so cells from another person are often rejected. An embryo cloned from the patient produces stem cells with the patient's own genes, so the body accepts them.",
    "Uses and objections":
      "Stem cells are useful because they could replace cells the body cannot replace. In type 1 diabetes the pancreas stops making enough insulin, and in paralysis nerve cells are damaged, so new cells grown from stem cells might one day take over. Bone marrow transplants already treat leukaemia, because the donor's stem cells rebuild a healthy blood supply. With plant meristems, a few cells can quickly grow into many identical plants, which saves rare species and gives farmers uniform crops. The risks come from the same flexibility. Cells grown in a lab can carry viruses into the patient, and cells that can still divide might form the wrong tissue or divide out of control. The ethical argument turns on how much moral value an early embryo has, which is why people who agree on the science can still disagree.",
  },
  "biology/enzymes-and-digestion": {
    "What enzymes are":
      "An enzyme works because of its shape. It is a protein, a long chain of amino acids folded into a precise three-dimensional form, and one small region of that shape, the active site, is the exact partner of the substrate. When the right substrate slots in, the enzyme holds it in a way that makes the reaction much easier, so it happens quickly and the products are released. The enzyme leaves unchanged and is free to take the next substrate, which is why a small amount of enzyme can process a huge number of molecules. A different molecule simply does not fit the site, which is why each enzyme is specific. Without enzymes, many of the body's reactions would only run at a useful rate at temperatures far too high for living cells.",
    "Temperature and pH":
      "Below the optimum, heat helps. The enzyme and substrate molecules move faster, so they collide more often and with more energy, and more substrate molecules fit into active sites each second. But the folded shape of the enzyme is held together by forces much weaker than the chain itself. As the temperature keeps rising, the enzyme vibrates more and more until those forces give way and the active site changes shape. Once it no longer matches the substrate, the rate drops sharply, and because the original folding has been lost, cooling cannot rebuild it. A pH far from the optimum disrupts the same weak forces, so it can denature an enzyme too. Each enzyme's optimum suits where it works, which is why stomach protease copes with acid while the enzymes of the small intestine need alkaline bile.",
    "The digestive enzymes":
      "Digestion is needed because most food arrives as large, insoluble molecules such as starch, proteins and fats, which are too big to pass through the gut wall into the blood. Enzymes break them into small soluble molecules that can be absorbed and then used for respiration or to build the body's own molecules. Because enzymes are specific, the body needs a different type for each food group: a carbohydrase cannot break down a protein, and a protease cannot break down a fat. Starch takes more than one step because it is a long chain of glucose units. Amylase cuts it into pairs of glucose called maltose, and another carbohydrase then splits each pair into single glucose molecules. Making each enzyme in more than one place means food keeps being digested as it moves along the gut.",
    "Bile and absorption":
      "Food leaves the stomach acidic, but the enzymes of the small intestine work best in slightly alkaline conditions. Bile is alkaline, so adding it neutralises the acid and brings the pH close to their optimum. Its second job is physical rather than chemical. Fat does not mix with water, so it clumps into large drops, and lipase can only work at the surface of a drop, where the fat meets the watery gut contents. Breaking one large drop into many tiny ones exposes far more surface, so many more lipase molecules can work at once. The gall bladder stores bile so that a larger amount can be released when fatty food arrives. Digestion has to be finished before absorption, because only small soluble molecules are able to pass through the thin wall of the small intestine and into the blood.",
  },
  "biology/plant-organisation": {
    "Plant tissues and the leaf":
      "A leaf is arranged around photosynthesis, with each tissue placed where it does the most good. Light arrives from above, so the upper epidermis is clear to let it through, and the palisade cells, with the most chloroplasts, sit directly underneath where the light is strongest. The waxy cuticle is waterproof, which slows evaporation from the leaf surface. Carbon dioxide has to reach the photosynthesising cells too, so the spongy layer below is full of air spaces, giving gases a large surface to diffuse into and out of the cells. The veins link the leaf to the rest of the plant: xylem brings the water needed for photosynthesis, and phloem carries the sugars made there to other parts. Meristems are where new cells come from, which is why a plant can keep adding new leaves, shoots and roots.",
    "Stomata and guard cells":
      "Stomata exist because the leaf needs carbon dioxide for photosynthesis, but any opening that lets carbon dioxide in also lets water vapour out. Guard cells let the plant manage that trade-off. When they have plenty of water they swell and curve apart, opening a gap between them. When water is short they lose water, go limp and close the gap, so the plant saves water at the cost of slowing photosynthesis. That is why a plant in a drought grows more slowly even if it survives. Having most stomata underneath helps because water evaporates faster from a warm surface in direct sunlight. The underside is shaded and cooler, so less water is lost through each open stoma than would be lost from the top of the leaf.",
    "Transpiration":
      "The pull comes from the top, not a pump at the bottom. As water evaporates from cells inside the leaf and diffuses out through the stomata, it is replaced by water drawn from the xylem. Water molecules stick to each other, so the xylem holds an unbroken column from leaf to root, and losing water at the top pulls the whole column upwards, like drinking through a straw. That draws water into the roots, where it enters root hair cells by osmosis. Each factor raises the rate by speeding up evaporation or diffusion. Heat gives water molecules more energy, wind and dry air carry water vapour away and keep the gradient steep, and light makes more stomata open. A potometer measures uptake because that is easy to see, and nearly all the water taken up is lost by transpiration.",
    "Translocation":
      "The two transport tissues differ because their jobs differ. Water only ever needs to go one way, up from the roots to the leaves, and it is pulled there by evaporation from the leaves, so xylem can be dead, empty tubes. Sugars, though, are made in the leaves but needed all over the plant, for respiration in the roots, for growing shoots, and for storage as starch in places such as potatoes. So the phloem has to move them wherever they are needed, up or down. That movement is not driven by evaporation, so the plant has to power it itself, and this is why phloem cells must stay alive: only living cells can supply energy from respiration. A leaf making sugar is a source and a growing root is a destination, and phloem links one to the other.",
  },
  "biology/the-microscope-and-practicals": {
    "Microscopes":
      "How much detail a microscope can show depends on what it uses to form the image. A light microscope cannot separate two points that are very close together, so beyond a certain magnification it only gives you a bigger blur. That is why ribosomes stayed invisible until electron microscopes arrived. An electron beam can separate points far closer together than light can, so its higher magnification reveals real new detail. The electrons only travel properly through a vacuum, and the specimen has to be specially treated and dried first, so nothing can be alive inside one. A light microscope needs only a lamp and glass lenses, which is why it stays cheap, portable and able to show living cells.",
    "The magnification equation":
      "Magnification simply compares two lengths: how big something looks in the image and how big it really is. Dividing one length by another cancels the units, which is why the answer has no units and is written as ×400. It is also why both lengths must be in the same unit first. If the image is measured in millimetres and the cell in micrometres, you are dividing numbers on two different scales, and the answer comes out a thousand times too big or too small. The rearranged version is the same relationship turned round, so you can work out a real size from an image you have measured. Lenses multiply in a chain, because the objective enlarges the specimen and the eyepiece then enlarges that already-enlarged image, so a ×10 eyepiece with a ×40 objective gives ×400.",
    "Preparing a slide":
      "Every step is about letting light pass cleanly through the specimen so you see the cells clearly. The specimen must be thin, ideally a single layer of cells, because light has to shine up through it; a thick piece looks dark and its layers overlap. Many cell parts are almost colourless, so a stain is added; it is taken up by particular structures and makes them stand out. Lowering the cover slip at an angle pushes air out ahead of it, because bubbles show up as dark rings that hide the cells. The lowest power objective shows the widest area, so it is easiest to find the specimen with it, and it sits furthest from the slide. Focusing by moving the lens away means a mistake only blurs the image, rather than cracking the slide or the lens.",
    "Working scientifically":
      "If you change only the independent variable and keep every control variable the same, any change in the dependent variable must have been caused by what you changed, which is what makes the test fair. Accuracy and precision can come apart. If a balance reads 2 g too high every time, your repeats agree closely, so they are precise, but none is near the true value, so they are not accurate. Repeatable and reproducible are checks of increasing strength: getting the same answer again rules out a one-off fluke, while someone else getting it with different equipment rules out a fault in your own kit or method. An anomalous result is left out of the mean because one error would drag the average away from the real pattern, and you say so because others need to judge that decision.",
  },
  "biology/communicable-disease": {
    "The four types of pathogen":
      "All four can pass from host to host, which is why their diseases are communicable, but each causes harm in its own way. Bacteria are complete living cells, so they can divide on their own in warm, moist tissue, and it is mainly the toxins they release that damage the tissue around them. A virus is not a cell and cannot copy itself on its own, so it gets inside one of your cells and makes that cell build new viruses. The cell bursts to release them, which is why damage grows as the infection spreads from cell to cell. The malaria protist lives inside the blood, so it needs a vector, an animal that carries them from one host to the next. Fungi spread by releasing spores, tiny cells that travel to new hosts.",
    "Named diseases you must know":
      "Sort these diseases by how each one travels, because the way a pathogen spreads decides how it can be stopped. Salmonella, gonorrhoea, measles and HIV have no vector, so prevention blocks the direct route: cooking food properly and vaccinating chickens, condoms for the sexually transmitted ones, and vaccination for measles. Malaria has an extra step, the mosquito, so killing mosquitoes or sleeping under nets breaks the chain without touching the protist. The type of pathogen decides the treatment, which is why gonorrhoea, a bacterium, can be treated with antibiotics while measles and HIV, both viruses, cannot. The two plant diseases do harm the same way. The pale patches caused by tobacco mosaic virus have less chlorophyll, and black spot makes leaves fall, so either way less light is absorbed, less photosynthesis happens and the plant grows less.",
    "How the body defends itself":
      "The defences are layered, so most pathogens never get far. Skin is a tough barrier, and the airways are lined with mucus that traps microorganisms, which the cilia then sweep up to the throat to be swallowed. Stomach acid kills most swallowed pathogens. These work against anything, which is why they are called non-specific. Pathogens that get past them meet white blood cells. Some white blood cells engulf and digest any pathogen they find. Others make antibodies, and each antibody has a shape that fits only one antigen, a particular molecule on the surface of a pathogen. That is why antibodies are specific, and why the right one takes days to make the first time. By clumping pathogens together, antibodies make them easier for the engulfing cells to destroy. Antitoxins tackle a separate problem, the poisons that bacteria release.",
    "Vaccination and drugs":
      "A vaccine works because the immune system is slow the first time it meets a pathogen. Making the right antibody takes days, long enough for a real pathogen to make you ill. A vaccine runs that first meeting safely, and the memory cells it leaves behind recognise the antigen next time and trigger antibody production almost at once. So the real pathogen is destroyed before it can multiply enough to cause symptoms. Herd immunity follows from the same idea: a pathogen needs a chain of people to infect, and if most are immune the chain keeps breaking. Antibiotics damage bacterial cells in ways that do not harm your own cells. Viruses are not cells and have none of those targets, and because they multiply inside your cells, a drug that destroyed them could damage your tissue too.",
  },
  "biology/non-communicable-disease": {
    "Risk factors":
      "A risk factor shifts the odds rather than deciding the outcome, because these diseases usually need several things to go wrong, and genes and chance play a part too. That is why some heavy smokers never get lung cancer while some non-smokers do. Carcinogens, such as chemicals in tobacco smoke and ionising radiation, damage DNA, which can lead to the uncontrolled division of cancer. Smoking also damages the lining of arteries, which raises the risk of heart disease. Obesity makes body cells respond less well to insulin, which is how Type 2 diabetes develops. Alcohol is broken down in the liver, so the liver takes the damage, and in the brain it slows nerve messages. In pregnancy, chemicals from smoke and alcohol pass from the mother's blood into the baby's while its organs are forming.",
    "Correlation and cause":
      "Two things can rise and fall together without one causing the other. Ice cream sales and drownings both go up in summer, but ice cream does not drown anyone; hot weather drives both. That is why a correlation alone is never enough, and why scientists look for a mechanism, an explanation of how one thing physically leads to the other. Smoking and lung cancer passed that test because chemicals in tobacco smoke were shown to damage the DNA in lung cells, which can trigger uncontrolled cell division. The quality of the study matters too. A small sample can show a pattern purely by chance, a short study can miss a disease that takes decades to appear, and a poorly controlled one cannot rule out other factors. So a large, long, well-controlled study makes a far stronger case.",
    "The cost of disease":
      "Non-communicable diseases cost so much because they are usually long-term. Someone with Type 2 diabetes or heart disease may need medicine, check-ups and hospital care for decades, and many can be managed but not cured. Every year of illness adds to the bill for treatment, to the working days lost, and to the human cost carried by the person, their family and their carers. Prevention is cheaper because it acts before any of that starts: helping someone stop smoking costs very little compared with years of cancer or heart treatment, which is why public health campaigns are worth funding. Poorer communities are hit harder because more of the risk factors cluster there, such as higher smoking rates and less access to affordable healthy food. So tackling them needs government policy, not just personal choices.",
    "Cancer":
      "Normally a cell divides only when the body needs new cells, and stops when enough have been made. Cancer begins when changes in a cell break those controls, so the cell keeps dividing and so do all the cells it produces, building a lump called a tumour. Whether a tumour is dangerous depends on whether its cells stay put. A benign tumour stays in one place, usually inside a membrane, so it is generally only harmful if it grows enough to press on an organ. A malignant tumour invades the tissue around it, and cells break away, travel in the blood and settle elsewhere, starting secondary tumours that disrupt other organs. Several risk factors cause those changes: chemicals in smoke, UV light and ionising radiation damage DNA, which is why they raise the chance that a cell loses control.",
  },
  "biology/photosynthesis": {
    "The reaction":
      "Photosynthesis builds a large, energy-rich molecule, glucose, out of small, low-energy ones, carbon dioxide and water. That building needs an input of energy, and light provides it, which is why the reaction is endothermic and needs light to happen. Chlorophyll is the green pigment that absorbs the light, and it is found inside chloroplasts, so that is where the reaction takes place. Count the atoms and the symbol equation balances: six carbons, twelve hydrogens and eighteen oxygens on each side, which is why six molecules each of carbon dioxide and water are needed for one glucose. Respiration uses the same substances the other way round, releasing the energy that photosynthesis stored. That is how energy from sunlight ends up powering almost every living thing, including the plant itself.",
    "What the glucose is used for":
      "Glucose is the plant's raw material as well as its fuel, because its atoms can be rebuilt into the other large molecules the plant needs. Some is respired, since every cell needs a constant energy supply, day and night. Glucose is soluble, so storing a lot of it would raise the concentration inside a cell and draw water in by osmosis. Joining glucose molecules into long starch chains keeps the same energy in an insoluble form that has no effect on water movement. Joined in a different way, glucose makes cellulose, the strong fibres of cell walls. Glucose contains only carbon, hydrogen and oxygen, but amino acids also need nitrogen, which is why the plant must absorb nitrate ions from the soil to make proteins. Lipids pack a lot of energy into a small mass, which suits seeds.",
    "Limiting factors":
      "Photosynthesis needs several things at once, light for energy, carbon dioxide as a raw material and chlorophyll to absorb the light, so it can only go as fast as the scarcest one allows. Think of a production line: extra parts of one kind are useless if another part is running short. That is why a graph rises and then flattens. At first the factor you are increasing is the shortage, so more of it speeds things up; once there is enough, something else becomes the shortage and the line levels off. Temperature is different because it works through enzymes. Warming speeds the reactions until the active sites change shape and the enzymes denature, so the rate drops instead of levelling off. Magnesium is needed to make chlorophyll, so a shortage of it lowers the rate.",
    "Using this in greenhouses":
      "A greenhouse lets a grower control the limiting factors that hold plants back outdoors. Artificial light means photosynthesis can continue after dark and on dull days, so the plants get more hours of growth. A paraffin heater is a neat solution because burning the fuel gives out heat and also produces carbon dioxide as a waste product, raising two factors with one piece of equipment. (Higher tier) The difficulty is that only the scarcest factor controls the rate. If light is already the limit, pumping in more carbon dioxide adds cost without adding growth, so a sensible grower works out which factor is limiting and raises that one. Each extra step costs more, so growers stop at the point where the extra crop would earn less than the extra fuel and equipment cost.",
    "Higher tier: the inverse square law":
      "Light from a lamp spreads out in all directions, so the same light is shared over a larger and larger area as you move away. That area grows with the square of the distance. Twice as far away, the light covers four times the area, so each part of the leaf gets a quarter as much. Three times as far, it covers nine times the area, so each part gets a ninth. This is why moving a lamp has such a large effect, and why distance on its own is not a measure of light intensity. In the pondweed practical you change the distance, so you calculate 1 ÷ d² for each distance to get a number that is proportional to light intensity. Plotting rate against that number, rather than distance, shows the true effect of light intensity.",
    "Higher tier: interacting limiting factors":
      "The rate of photosynthesis is set by whichever factor runs short first, not by all of them added together. If the light would allow a high rate but the carbon dioxide only a low one, carbon dioxide is in control, and extra light changes nothing. That is why, on a graph of rate against light intensity, the rising part of a curve is where light is limiting, and the flat part is where another factor has taken over. If two curves are drawn at different carbon dioxide concentrations, the one with more carbon dioxide levels off higher, because extra light kept raising the rate for longer before carbon dioxide became the shortage. To name the limiting factor at a point, ask whether the curve is still rising there.",
  },
  "biology/respiration": {
    "Aerobic respiration":
      "Glucose is a chemical store of energy, and respiration releases that energy by breaking glucose down into carbon dioxide and water, which store far less. The difference is transferred to the surroundings, which is why the reaction is exothermic and why you warm up when you exercise. The symbol equation is the photosynthesis equation turned round, because the same atoms end up back where they started. Oxygen is what lets the breakdown go all the way to carbon dioxide and water. That is why aerobic respiration releases so much more energy per glucose than anaerobic respiration, which stops part-way and leaves energy locked in lactic acid or ethanol. Cells need energy every moment for processes that never stop, so respiration has to run continuously, and that is why very active cells such as muscle cells contain large numbers of mitochondria.",
    "What the energy is used for":
      "Living things are constantly building and moving, and both need energy, which is why respiration never stops. Joining small molecules into large ones, such as amino acids into proteins, or glucose into starch and cellulose in plants, only happens with an input of energy. Plants also use energy to turn the nitrate they absorb into amino acids. Muscle contraction needs energy too, so harder exercise means muscle cells have to respire faster. Mammals and birds keep their bodies warmer than their surroundings, and they are losing heat all the time, so some of the energy released by respiration warms the body and makes up for that loss. All of these reactions together make up the metabolism, and because so many of them need energy, respiration is the reaction the rest depend on.",
    "Anaerobic respiration":
      "Without oxygen, a cell cannot break glucose all the way down to carbon dioxide and water. It can still break glucose down part of the way, which releases a small amount of energy, but the product, lactic acid in muscle or ethanol in yeast and plant cells, still holds most of the energy that was in the glucose. That is why anaerobic respiration releases so much less energy per glucose. It is still worth having because it keeps cells supplied when oxygen cannot arrive fast enough, as in a sprint, or for yeast in a sealed container with no air. The products of fermentation are what make it useful to people: the carbon dioxide forms bubbles that make bread dough rise, and the ethanol is the alcohol in beer and wine.",
    "Exercise and oxygen debt":
      "Working muscles respire faster, so they need more oxygen and glucose and produce more carbon dioxide. A faster heart rate sends more blood through the muscles each minute, and faster, deeper breathing loads more oxygen into that blood and gets rid of the extra carbon dioxide. In very hard exercise even this cannot deliver oxygen fast enough, so muscles top up their energy supply by respiring anaerobically. Lactic acid then builds up, and the muscles become fatigued and contract less efficiently. (Higher tier) When you stop, the lactic acid still has to be removed. Blood carries it to the liver, which converts it back into glucose, and that process needs oxygen. This extra oxygen is the oxygen debt, which is why you keep breathing hard and your heart keeps beating fast for a while after the exercise has ended.",
  },
  "biology/the-nervous-system": {
    "The pathway":
      "Every response depends on information travelling in one direction, which is why the order of the pathway never changes. A receptor is a cell specialised to detect one kind of stimulus, so the cells in your eyes respond to light but not to sound. The receptor starts an electrical impulse, and a sensory neurone carries it to the CNS. The brain and spinal cord are where impulses from many receptors arrive, so this is where the information is processed and a response is coordinated. A motor neurone then carries an impulse out to an effector, the part that carries out the response: a muscle contracting, or a gland releasing its secretion. Information goes in along sensory neurones, is sorted in the CNS, and goes out along motor neurones.",
    "The reflex arc":
      "Normally an impulse from a receptor travels up to the conscious brain, where you weigh it up before acting, and that takes time. In a reflex the impulse takes a shortcut. It reaches a relay neurone in the spinal cord or an unconscious part of the brain, and the relay neurone passes it straight on to a motor neurone. Fewer neurones means fewer synapses to cross and no decision to make, so the response happens before you are even aware of the stimulus. That speed is the whole point. By the time you feel the pain from a hot pan, your hand has already moved, so the damage is smaller. Because reflexes skip conscious thought, they are very hard to stop, which is why you blink even when you know the object cannot reach you.",
    "Neurones and synapses":
      "An impulse travels quickly along a neurone because it is an electrical signal passing along one long, continuous cell. A synapse breaks that continuity. The impulse cannot jump the gap, so when it reaches the end of the axon it causes a chemical, the neurotransmitter, to be released. The chemical diffuses across the gap and attaches to the next neurone, which sets off a brand new impulse there. Diffusion is much slower than an electrical impulse, so every synapse adds a short delay, and that is why a pathway with many synapses responds more slowly than one with few. The gap is still worth having. The chemical is only released from one side, so signals can only pass across a synapse in one direction, which keeps messages flowing the right way through the pathway.",
    "Reaction time practicals":
      "A falling ruler speeds up in a predictable way, so the distance it drops before you catch it depends only on how long it was falling. That is why a distance on the ruler can be converted into a time using a table or a formula. The result only measures reaction time if nothing else can change the catch distance. A warning lets you anticipate the drop, and starting with your fingers at a different mark means the reading no longer matches the time, so those are kept the same. Single catches vary a lot because people lose concentration or guess, so repeating and taking a mean gives a more reliable value. Caffeine is a stimulant that makes the nervous system respond faster, which is why a shorter catch distance is the expected result.",
  },
  "biology/hormones-and-homeostasis": {
    "Homeostasis and the two control systems":
      "Almost everything a cell does depends on enzymes, which only work well in a narrow range of conditions. If body temperature drifts too far, or the blood holds too much or too little glucose or water, cells stop working properly, so these levels are held close to a set value. Holding a level steady means measuring it and correcting it, which is why every control system needs something to detect the change, something to decide on a response, and something to carry it out.\n\nThe nervous and endocrine systems suit different jobs because of how their signals travel. An impulse runs along a neurone to one particular muscle or gland, so it arrives almost instantly but ends quickly. A hormone spreads through the blood, so it arrives more slowly, reaches many organs at once, and keeps acting for longer.",
    "The endocrine glands":
      "An endocrine gland is an organ that makes a hormone and releases it straight into the blood. Each hormone travels everywhere the blood goes, but only affects target organs whose cells can respond to it, which is how one bloodstream can carry many different messages without them getting mixed up. The pituitary is called the master gland because many of its hormones target other glands rather than the rest of the body, so it controls how much of their own hormones those glands release. It sits just below the brain, which lets the brain influence the whole system through it. The other glands each control something that involves many organs at once: the blood glucose level, how fast the body uses energy, readiness for danger, and the changes of puberty and reproduction.",
    "Controlling blood glucose":
      "Cells need a steady supply of glucose for respiration, but too much is also harmful, so the level is kept within narrow limits. The pancreas acts as both receptor and coordination centre: it detects the glucose concentration and releases the hormone that corrects it. After a meal glucose rises, so insulin makes cells take glucose in from the blood, and liver and muscle cells store the extra as glycogen. (Higher tier) Between meals glucose falls, so the pancreas releases glucagon, which makes the liver convert glycogen back into glucose and release it. The two hormones push in opposite directions, which is what keeps the level steady.\n\nDiabetes happens when the insulin side fails. In Type 1 there is too little insulin, so injections replace it. In Type 2 cells respond poorly, so diet and exercise help by stopping glucose building up.",
    "Higher tier: negative feedback":
      "Negative feedback works like a heating thermostat: when the level moves too far one way, the response pushes it back, and once it is back the response eases off. TSH from the pituitary makes the thyroid produce thyroxine. If thyroxine falls, more TSH is released, so the thyroid makes more. Once thyroxine rises above normal it inhibits TSH release, so the thyroid slows down. The product of the loop is what controls the loop, which is why the level stays steady without anything else stepping in. That suits thyroxine, because metabolic rate and growth need steady control over months and years. Adrenaline has a different job. It is not correcting a level that has drifted but responding to danger, so it is released when you are frightened or stressed and prepares the muscles for fight or flight.",
  },
  "biology/the-circulatory-system": {
    "The heart and double circulation":
      "Blood loses a lot of pressure as it squeezes through the tiny capillaries in the lungs. If it then carried straight on to the rest of the body, it would move slowly and oxygen would reach active tissues too slowly. A double circulation solves this by sending blood back to the heart after the lungs, so it can be pumped a second time at high pressure. That is why the heart is really two pumps side by side, kept separate so that oxygenated and deoxygenated blood do not mix. The right side only has to push blood to the lungs, which are close by, while the left side has to push it around the whole body, so the left ventricle needs the thickest muscle. Both sides beat in time because the pacemaker sends out the signal for every beat.",
    "The blood vessels":
      "Each vessel's structure matches the pressure inside it. Arteries receive blood straight from the ventricles in powerful surges, so they need thick muscle to withstand the pressure and elastic tissue that stretches with each beat and springs back. By the time blood has passed through the capillaries its pressure is low, so veins do not need thick walls. Instead they have a wide lumen, which lets slow, low-pressure blood flow easily, and valves, because with so little pressure behind it blood could slip backwards. Diffusion is only fast over short distances and large areas, so capillary walls one cell thick and huge branching networks mean every cell is close to a capillary. Arteries are named by direction, not oxygen: the pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood away from the heart to the lungs.",
    "What blood is made of":
      "Blood has two jobs, transporting substances and defending the body, and each part suits one of them. Plasma does most of the carrying because so many substances dissolve in it, such as glucose, carbon dioxide, urea and hormones, and it also carries the cells along. Oxygen is the exception, because very little dissolves in plasma, so red blood cells carry it bound to haemoglobin. Everything about a red blood cell helps with that: with no nucleus there is more space for haemoglobin, and the dented biconcave shape gives more surface for oxygen to diffuse in and out. White blood cells defend in different ways: some engulf and digest pathogens, while others make antibodies that target them or antitoxins that neutralise their toxins. Platelets help a clot form at a wound, which stops blood loss and helps keep pathogens out.",
    "Heart problems and treatments":
      "The heart beats continuously, so its muscle needs a constant supply of oxygen for respiration, delivered by the coronary arteries that run over its surface. When fatty deposits narrow those arteries, less blood gets through, so the muscle cannot respire enough and can be damaged. Each treatment tackles a different part of the problem, which is why each has different drawbacks. A stent is a mesh tube that props one narrowed section open, so flow improves at once, but nothing stops fatty material building up elsewhere. Statins lower blood cholesterol, which slows down the formation of new deposits, but only for as long as the person keeps taking them, which is why side effects matter. Valve replacements and artificial pacemakers fix different faults: a leaking or stiff valve, or a heart that does not keep a regular rhythm.",
    "Rate of blood flow":
      "A rate is always an amount divided by the time it took, so the units come straight from the calculation: cubic centimetres divided by minutes gives cm³ per minute. That is why the time must be in the unit you want before you divide. If 1500 cm³ passes in 30 seconds, change 30 seconds to 0.5 minutes first, which gives 3000 cm³ per minute. Dividing by 30 instead gives 50 cm³ per second, which is true but in different units, so it is easy to be out by a factor of 60. Comparing with a known value catches slips. A resting heart pumps about 5 dm³ (5000 cm³) a minute, so a whole-heart answer far from that has probably gone wrong. During exercise the figure rises, because the heart beats faster and pumps more blood with each beat.",
  },
  "biology/dna-and-reproduction": {
    "DNA and genes":
      "DNA can hold a huge amount of information because it is a polymer, a long chain of repeating units, each containing one of four bases. The bases work like letters: on their own they mean nothing, but their order along a gene spells out the order of amino acids in a protein. A different order of amino acids folds into a different protein, so the base sequence decides which protein is made, and proteins such as enzymes then shape how the cell and the organism develop. The two strands are held together by their bases, and since A only fits with T and C only with G, each strand fixes the order of the other. That is why DNA can be copied accurately: the strands separate and each one acts as a template for building a new partner.",
    "Why the genome matters":
      "Knowing the genome means knowing the base sequence of the human genes, which is useful because many differences between people come down to small differences in that sequence. If people with a particular disease tend to share a certain version of a gene, that gene is probably involved, so doctors can test for it, predict who is at risk and look for better treatments for inherited disorders. Migration can be traced for a similar reason: DNA is passed down the generations with small changes building up over time, so populations that share more of their DNA are more closely related, and the pattern shows how groups spread around the world. The concerns arise because the information is personal and permanent. You cannot change your genes, so data about them could be used unfairly against you.",
    "Sexual and asexual reproduction":
      "It comes down to how the new cells are made. In asexual reproduction they are made only by mitosis, which copies the parent's DNA, so every offspring is a clone. In sexual reproduction each parent makes gametes, and when two fuse the offspring gets a new combination of alleles from both, which is why siblings differ. Each method wins in different situations. Cloning is quick and needs no partner, so a well-adapted organism in a stable habitat can quickly fill it with equally well-suited offspring. But if conditions change, say a new disease appears, identical offspring may all be equally vulnerable. Variation makes it more likely that some offspring survive and pass on their alleles, which is how natural selection works. Strawberries, with runners and seeds, get the speed of one method and the variation of the other.",
    "Meiosis":
      "Meiosis halves the chromosome number because fertilisation doubles it. If gametes kept all 46, every generation would have twice as many chromosomes as the last. With 23 in each, egg and sperm together restore 46. Body cells hold their chromosomes in pairs, and meiosis gives each gamete just one chromosome from every pair. The cell copies its chromosomes and then divides twice, which is why four cells are made. Which chromosome of each pair ends up in a particular gamete is random, so the gametes have different combinations from each other and from the parent.\n\nAfter fertilisation the job changes. The embryo needs huge numbers of cells that all carry the same genetic information as the fertilised egg, so from then on cells divide by mitosis, which copies the full set. Only later do those cells differentiate into specialised types.",
  },
  "biology/genetics-and-inheritance": {
    "The vocabulary":
      "Your body cells carry two copies of most genes, one on each chromosome of a pair, one inherited from each parent. Those two copies can be the same allele or different ones, and the words describe each case. If both are the same, the person is homozygous and there is no conflict: BB shows the dominant characteristic and bb shows the recessive one. If they differ, the person is heterozygous and the dominant allele is the one you see, which is why a single capital letter is enough to show it. The recessive allele is still there in Bb, just hidden, so genotype and phenotype are not the same thing: BB and Bb look identical but carry different alleles. That hidden copy is exactly what a carrier has, which is how a recessive disorder can pass unnoticed through a family.",
    "Punnett squares":
      "Each parent passes on only one of their two alleles, and each is equally likely to end up in a given egg or sperm. The top and side list the possible gametes, so each box is one equally likely way a sperm and egg could combine, which is why counting boxes gives a ratio. With Bb × Bb, three of the four boxes contain at least one dominant B, so only bb shows the recessive phenotype. With Bb × bb, the bb parent can only give b, so the outcome depends entirely on the other parent, giving 1:1. Every child comes from a separate fertilisation, so each has the same chances whatever happened before, just as three heads in a row does not make the next coin toss more likely to be tails.",
    "Sex determination":
      "Sex is inherited like any other characteristic, except that the pair of chromosomes involved is the sex chromosomes. When gametes form, each one gets one chromosome from every pair. A woman's pair is XX, so every egg she makes gets an X and there is no other option. A man's pair is XY, so half of his sperm get the X and half get the Y. That is why the sperm decides the outcome: an X sperm fertilising the egg gives XX, a girl, and a Y sperm gives XY, a boy. Because the two kinds of sperm are made in equal numbers, each pregnancy has the same 50% chance of either, which is exactly what the Punnett square shows. As with any probability, a family can still have several children of the same sex in a row.",
    "Inherited disorders and screening":
      "Whether a disorder can appear without warning depends on whether its allele is dominant or recessive. Polydactyly is dominant, so anyone with even one copy has extra digits, which means a parent who passes it on has the condition too and it cannot stay hidden. Cystic fibrosis is recessive, so a person needs two copies to be affected. Someone with one copy is a carrier with no symptoms, so two carriers can be unaware until they have an affected child, and each of their children has a 1 in 4 chance. That is why screening exists: a test can reveal what family history cannot. Much of the debate is not about whether the tests work but about what people then do with the result, which is why the arguments involve cost, risk and personal values as well as biology.",
  },
  "biology/variation-and-evolution": {
    "Variation":
      "Genes and environment work together because genes set the range an organism could reach, while conditions such as diet, light or temperature decide where in that range it ends up. A plant with genes for tallness will still be short in poor soil.\n\nThe genetic side is reshuffled every generation. Meiosis gives each gamete a different mix of alleles, and which sperm meets which egg is down to chance, so siblings differ even though they share parents. But shuffling only recombines alleles that already exist. A mutation is the only way a genuinely new allele appears. Most mutations change nothing you can see, because they either leave the protein unchanged or change it so slightly that it still works the same way. Occasionally the new protein suits the environment better, and natural selection then has something new to favour.",
    "Natural selection":
      "Natural selection happens because more offspring are produced than the environment can support, so individuals compete for food, mates and space, and many die before they breed. Which ones survive is not random: if a variation helps, such as thicker fur in a cold climate, its owner is more likely to live long enough to reproduce. If that characteristic is genetic, its offspring are likely to inherit the allele behind it. Each generation, a slightly larger share of the population carries the helpful allele, and over hundreds or thousands of generations those small shifts add up to a changed species. No individual ever changes to fit its surroundings; it is the population that changes, one generation at a time. Darwin's idea was slow to be accepted partly because nobody yet knew how characteristics were inherited.",
    "Evidence and objections":
      "Fossils count as evidence because rock builds up in layers, with deeper layers usually older, so fossils in order show how life has changed. The gaps exist because fossils only form in unusual conditions. Hard parts such as bone and shell can be slowly replaced by minerals, but soft bodies usually decay first, and many early fossils were destroyed by geological activity. Antibiotic resistance is strong evidence because bacteria breed so fast that you can watch selection happen within years.\n\nLamarck was wrong because changes to your body during life, like muscles built by training, do not alter the DNA in your sex cells, so they cannot be passed on. Darwin could see that characteristics were inherited but could not explain how. Mendel had shown that inheritance works through separate units, now called genes, but Darwin never knew of it.",
    "Antibiotic resistance":
      "The antibiotic does not cause resistance; it reveals it. The mutation arose by chance before the drug was ever used, and until the drug arrives, the resistant one is just one among millions. Once the drug kills the rest, it has the food and space to itself, and because bacteria can divide as often as every 20 minutes in good conditions, its descendants soon dominate. Each of them inherits the resistance gene, so the population is now resistant.\n\nThat is why overuse matters: every unnecessary course is another round of selection. Antibiotics do nothing to viruses, so prescribing them for a cold only selects for resistant bacteria already living in your body. Finishing the course keeps the drug working long enough to kill even the least sensitive bacteria, instead of leaving them alive with their competition gone.",
    "Speciation":
      "The test for a species is fertile offspring because interbreeding is what keeps a species one shared gene pool: as long as members breed together, a new allele can spread through all of them. Isolation breaks that link. A mountain range, a stretch of sea or a change in a river's course can split a population, and from then on a mutation in one group can never reach the other. Each group also faces its own climate, food and predators, so different variants survive in each. New mutations and selection for different features push the two gene pools further apart every generation. Eventually the differences are so great that, even if the groups meet again, they either cannot mate or their offspring are infertile. That inability to produce fertile offspring is exactly what makes them separate species.",
  },
  "biology/classification-and-selection": {
    "Traditional classification":
      "Linnaeus grouped organisms by what he could see and measure, such as body plan, number of legs or flower structure, because that was the only evidence available in the 1700s. The groups nest inside one another, rather like a postal address: many species share a genus, many genera share a family, and so on up to kingdom, so organisms in the same small group share more features than those that only share a large one.\n\nThe binomial name solves a practical problem. Common names vary between places and languages, so a two-part Latin name gives everyone one label, and it tells you the genus straight away. The weakness was the evidence itself. Organisms can look alike without being closely related, so once microscopes revealed internal structures and chemistry revealed molecules, some groupings had to change.",
    "The three-domain system":
      "Appearance can mislead because unrelated organisms can end up looking alike, and microscopic organisms have very few visible features to compare. Molecules such as RNA and DNA change slowly by mutation over time, so two groups with very similar sequences must have split from a common ancestor recently, while very different sequences mean a long time apart.\n\nWhen Woese compared sequences from single-celled organisms without a nucleus, which had all been grouped as bacteria, he found that the archaea were fundamentally different from true bacteria in their chemistry. That meant the biggest division in living things was not between kingdoms such as plants and animals, but between three domains, with every organism that has a nucleus, from a mushroom to you, in just one of them. Scientists accepted it because the new data explained relationships better.",
    "Evolutionary trees":
      "Each branch point on an evolutionary tree stands for an ancestral population that split in two, and everything along the branches after it is descended from that ancestor. Two species that branch from a recent point have had less time to build up separate mutations, so their DNA is still very similar, which is exactly how living species are placed. Extinct species usually have no DNA left to sample, so scientists use the features and age of their fossils instead.\n\nPosition on the page can mislead you because a tree can be swivelled at any branch point without changing its meaning, like turning one arm of a hanging mobile. Only the route back through the branches tells you how related they are: the more recent the branch point they share, the closer the relationship.",
    "Selective breeding and genetic engineering":
      "Selective breeding works because characteristics such as milk yield are partly controlled by genes. Breeding the best parents makes their offspring likely to inherit those alleles, and repeating the choice each generation keeps concentrating them.\n\nThe cost comes from always choosing from a few closely related individuals. Other alleles are lost, so the population becomes genetically very similar, and a disease that one cannot resist is likely to affect them all. Close relatives also tend to carry the same harmful recessive allele, so their offspring are more likely to inherit two copies and show the defect. Genetic engineering is faster and more precise because it moves one useful gene directly, even between species. The genetic code works the same way in all organisms, so a bacterium given the human insulin gene makes human insulin.",
  },
  "biology/ecosystems-and-food-chains": {
    "Communities and interdependence":
      "No species gets everything it needs on its own, which is why the living part of an area works as a connected community rather than a collection of separate populations. A fox depends on rabbits, rabbits depend on grass, and many plants depend on insects to carry their pollen. That is why removing one species spreads: if rabbits vanished, foxes would go hungry and the grass would grow taller, shading out smaller plants. Competition comes from the same limits seen from the other side. Any area holds only so much of each resource, so organisms that need the same thing compete for it. Plants need light and water to photosynthesise and mineral ions to grow, while animals need food to survive and mates to reproduce. In a stable community these pressures balance out, so population sizes stay roughly constant.",
    "Abiotic and biotic factors":
      "Abiotic factors act through the organism's own processes: lower light intensity slows photosynthesis, so plants grow less and the animals that eat them have less food; a fall in temperature slows enzyme-controlled reactions; low oxygen in water starves fish of what they need to respire. Biotic factors act through other organisms: a new predator or pathogen kills more individuals, and a better competitor takes the food first.\n\nAdaptations are the features natural selection has favoured in those conditions. Structural ones are body parts, such as a fennec fox's large ears for losing heat; behavioural ones are actions, such as migrating; functional ones are processes inside the body. Extremophiles show how far this can go, with enzymes that keep working at temperatures that would denature ours.",
    "Food chains and biomass":
      "Food chains start with a producer because photosynthesis is how energy from sunlight enters the living world: plants and algae lock it into glucose, which they use to build biomass. Every consumer depends on that store, and most of it never reaches the next level. Some is not eaten at all, such as roots and bones, and some that is eaten cannot be digested and leaves as faeces. Of what is absorbed, much of the glucose is used in respiration to keep the animal alive, so it leaves as carbon dioxide and water and its energy ends up as heat. Only the small part built into new tissue can be passed on. With roughly a tenth surviving each step, the fifth level gets only a ten-thousandth of what the producers made, too little to support another population of predators.",
    "Cycling materials and decay":
      "Earth has a fixed amount of carbon and water, so life can only continue if the same atoms are used again and again. Plants build carbon dioxide from the air into glucose and then into their bodies. Animals eat the plants, and every organism, plants included, returns some of that carbon as carbon dioxide when it respires.\n\nWhen things die, bacteria and fungi release enzymes onto dead material, absorb the digested products and respire them, so carbon goes back to the air and mineral ions go back to the soil for plants to reuse. Decay depends on conditions because it is carried out by living microorganisms. Warmth speeds up their enzymes, up to a point, moisture lets them grow and digest, and oxygen lets them respire aerobically. That is why compost heaps are kept damp and turned.",
    "Sampling":
      "You cannot count every daisy in a field, so you count small measured areas and scale up. That only works if your sample is typical of the whole field. People tend to drop quadrats where plants look interesting or easy to reach, which overestimates them, so random coordinates take the choice out of your hands. The calculation is just scaling: if a 0.25 square metre quadrat holds 4 daisies on average, each square metre holds about 16, and a 100 square metre field holds about 1600.\n\nAny single quadrat might land on an unusually dense or bare patch, but those flukes even out when you average many. A transect is used when you expect a change, such as from a hedge into open grass, because sampling at regular points along a line shows how numbers change with conditions like light.",
  },
  "biology/biodiversity-and-human-impact": {
    "Biodiversity":
      "Stability comes from having more than one option. In a varied ecosystem a predator usually has several prey species, so if one declines it can switch to another rather than starve, and the damage stops spreading. In an ecosystem with few species, each one depends heavily on a few others, so a single loss can bring down several populations in turn. Disease follows a similar logic. Many pathogens infect only one species or a few close relatives, so when species are mixed together there are fewer suitable hosts close by for a pathogen to spread between.\n\nThe loss is permanent because each species carries a unique set of alleles built up over millions of years of mutation and selection. Once the last individuals die, those alleles are gone and cannot be recreated.",
    "Population and waste":
      "Waste grows faster than the population alone would suggest, because each person is also using more. A higher standard of living means more food, energy, transport and manufactured goods per person, and each of those produces waste somewhere.\n\nEach kind of pollution harms living things in its own way. Fertiliser washed into a river supplies nitrates that let algae grow rapidly, which spread over the surface and block light from the plants below; when those plants die, bacteria decomposing them use up the dissolved oxygen, so fish suffocate. Sulfur dioxide dissolves in rainwater to form an acid, which damages leaves and can make lakes too acidic for many species. Toxic chemicals poison the organisms that take them in. Because pollution travels through air, water and soil, its effects reach far beyond where it started, which is how it can reduce biodiversity across a whole area.",
    "Land use and deforestation":
      "When land is built on, quarried, farmed or filled with waste, the organisms living there lose their habitat, and a field of one crop supports far fewer species than the mixed habitat it replaced.\n\nPeat bogs show how land use can affect the climate too. Peat forms from plants that died in waterlogged bogs, where there is too little oxygen for decomposers to break them down, so their carbon was never returned to the air and has built up over millennia. Draining or digging the peat lets oxygen in, so microorganisms finally decay it and release carbon dioxide, and burning it releases the carbon even faster. Forests store carbon in the same way and keep removing carbon dioxide by photosynthesis, so cutting and burning them both adds carbon dioxide and removes something that was absorbing it.",
    "Climate change and conservation":
      "Carbon dioxide and methane are greenhouse gases: they let sunlight through but absorb some of the heat the Earth gives off, so more of them keeps more energy in the atmosphere and raises the average temperature. Water expands as it warms and ice on land melts, so sea levels rise and low-lying habitats flood. Each species can only live within a certain range of conditions, so as the climate shifts, species move to new areas or change when they migrate, and those that cannot move or adapt die out.\n\nConservation measures target the causes of loss: breeding programmes rebuild numbers, protected habitats give species space, and hedgerows and field margins give wildlife places to feed and shelter. They conflict with other needs because land and money used for conservation cannot also be used for farming, homes or jobs.",
  },
  "biology/biology-exam-practice": {
    "Command words":
      "Each command word asks for a different kind of thinking. A state question checks that you can recall one fact, so reasoning earns nothing extra. Describe asks what is happening: for a graph, that means the trend plus real numbers read from it. Explain asks for the cause behind the pattern, which is why 'because' is such a good test. If it is missing, you have probably only described.\n\nCompare needs both things side by side because the mark is for the difference, and you cannot show a difference by talking about only one of them. Evaluate needs a conclusion because weighing up only counts if you then decide. Calculations need working because the marks are split into steps, so choosing the right formula and substituting correctly each score even if a slip spoils the final answer.",
    "Six-mark questions":
      "A six-mark answer is judged as a whole because it tests whether you understand how ideas connect, not just whether you know them. It is placed in a level: a few relevant facts sit at the bottom, a clear and logical chain of reasoning at the top. That is why linking words matter. 'The temperature rose above the optimum, so the active site changed shape, which means the substrate no longer fits' is a chain; three separate facts are not.\n\nPlanning first helps because it lets you put your points in a logical order before you start. Precise vocabulary counts because a vague word like 'damaged' could mean several things, while 'denatured' says exactly what happened. The context matters because the question is asking you to apply the biology to that situation, not just recite it.",
    "Required practicals":
      "The practicals can appear in any paper because they are part of the content, not an extra. Learning each one in the same four parts works because every question about an experiment comes back to them. Once you know which variable was changed, which was measured and which were kept the same, you can explain why the method is done the way it is and predict the results.\n\nKnowing the sources of error lets you suggest improvements, because an improvement is simply something that removes one of them. An anomaly stands out because it does not fit the pattern the other results make, which usually points to a measuring mistake. The food test colours are worth knowing exactly because each reagent gives its colour change with one type of food molecule, so the colour tells you what is present.",
    "Habits that cost marks":
      "Most of these habits say something not quite true. An enzyme is a protein, not a living thing, so it cannot die; heat changes the shape of its active site, which is what denatured means. Respiration cannot make energy, because energy is never created, only transferred; it releases the energy stored in glucose. A number like 4 means nothing without units. Magnification is the exception because it is image size divided by real size, so the units cancel.\n\nThe organism matters because different organisms really do behave differently: anaerobic respiration produces lactic acid in muscle but ethanol and carbon dioxide in yeast. The data is given because the question wants you to use it, so an answer from memory can be true but still miss the point. A clock check works because six marks are worth roughly six minutes.",
  },
};
